.TH TASKSET 1 "October, 2026"

.SH NAME
\fBtaskset\fP \- Set processor affinity

.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtaskset\fP \fImask\fP \fIcommand\fP [\fIargument\fP...]
.br
\fBtaskset\fP \fB\-p\fP [\fImask\fP] \fIpid\fP

.SH DESCRIPTION
View or set the processor affinity of a process or new command.
.PP
In the first form, run a new command with the affinity described
by \fImask\fP, which is a hexadecimal representation of the bitmap
of available processor cores.
.PP
In the second form, if no \fImask\fP is provided, the current
affinity of \fIpid\fP will be queried and displayed, and if
a \fImask\fP is provided the affinity for \fIpid\fP will be
updated.

.SS Options

.TP
.B \-p
View or change the affinity for an existing process.

.SH STANDARDS
This utility is vaguely based on the one from \fButil\-linux\fP,
but with fewer options.

.SH NOTES
Processor affinity is per-thread, is inhereted from the parent process
on \fBfork\fP, and is maintained through \fBexecve\fP.
.PP
The affinity must include at least one processor that exists when
it is set.
Misaka does not allow the affinity of a thread to be empty; as a special
case, if 0 is specified as the \fImask\fP, the processor affinity
will be unset - that is, any processor will be available to that thread.
